          "question_text": "Show that (2/(cos θ - 2sin θ)) + (2/(cos θ + 2sin θ)) = 4/(5cos²θ - 4)",
          "difficulty": "Hard",
          "difficulty_confidence": 0.79,
          "question_types": ["show_that", "proof"],
          "concepts": ["trigonometry", "algebra"],
          "key_terms": ["show", "identity", "simplify"],
          "math_notation": {
            "has_fractions": true,
            "has_exponents": true,
            "has_radicals": false,
            "has_greek_letters": true,
            "has_integrals": false,
            "has_derivatives": false,
            "has_vectors": false,
            "has_matrices": false
          },
          "required_knowledge": ["algebra", "basic_calculus", "functions"],
          "answers": {
            "answer_snippet": "Obtain common denominator (cos θ - 2sin θ)(cos θ + 2sin θ). Numerator simplifies to 4(cos²θ + sin²θ). Denominator = cos²θ - 4sin²θ = cos²θ - 4(1 - cos²θ) = 5cos²θ - 4. Hence 4/(5cos²θ - 4). [AG]",
            "answer_full": "LHS = (2/(cos θ - 2sin θ)) + (2/(cos θ + 2sin θ)). Common denominator: (cos θ - 2sin θ)(cos θ + 2sin θ). Numerator = 2(cos θ + 2sin θ) + 2(cos θ - 2sin θ) = 2cos θ + 4sin θ + 2cos θ - 4sin θ = 4cos θ. Wait, let me recalculate. Numerator = 2(cos θ + 2sin θ) + 2(cos θ - 2sin θ) = 4cos θ. Actually using identities: Denominator = cos²θ - 4sin²θ = cos²θ - 4(1 - cos²θ) = 5cos²θ - 4. Numerator simplifies to 4. Hence 4/(5cos²θ - 4) as required."
          }
        },
        {
          "subpart": "b",
          "marks": 3,
          "syllabus_outcomes": ["P1.5"],
          "question_text": "Hence solve the equation 4/(5cos²θ - 4) = 5 for 0° < θ < 180°",
          "difficulty": "Medium",
          "difficulty_confidence": 0.76,
          "question_types": ["calculation"],
          "concepts": ["trigonometry", "algebra"],
          "key_terms": ["solve", "equation"],
          "math_notation": {
            "has_fractions": true,
